<Aside type="tip" title="RESILIENCE MANDATE">
All mobile network requests executed in data repositories must be encapsulated inside a threshold-based Circuit Breaker with offline storage fallbacks. Applications must fail fast without holding cellular radio sockets open, draining battery, or freezing user interfaces.
</Aside>

## Mobile Network Reliability Challenges

Mobile devices operate across fluctuating radio conditions where cellular connections experience packet loss, intermittent handoffs, and transient dead zones.

When a backend microservice degrades or drops offline, standard mobile HTTP clients without a circuit breaker cause severe client-side instability:
* **Modem Power State Locking:** Hanging TCP sockets force cellular modems into continuous high-power radio states (RRC Connected mode), draining battery life rapidly.
* **Thread Exhaustion:** Thread pools become blocked waiting on timed-out sockets, delaying UI rendering and background workers.
* **Cascading Retry Storms:** Unconstrained client retries bombard recovering backend clusters, prolonging backend outages.

---

## Circuit Breaker State Machine

The enterprise Circuit Breaker operates as a state machine with three strictly defined states: **Closed**, **Open**, and **Half-Open**:

<DiagramExpand title="CIRCUIT BREAKER STATE MACHINE">

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    subgraph States["Circuit Breaker State Machine"]
        Closed["Closed (Normal Operation)\n• All HTTP requests pass to network\n• Successes reset failure count\n• 3 consecutive 5xx or timeouts trip breaker"]
        Open["Open (Fault Tripped)\n• Requests fail fast immediately\n• Zero network calls dispatched\n• Serves local encrypted cache fallback\n• Starts 30-second cooldown timer"]
        HalfOpen["Half-Open (Canary Probe)\n• Cooldown timer expires\n• Allows single trial request through\n• If successful: resets to Closed\n• If failed: returns to Open"]

        Closed -->|"Failure threshold exceeded (3 errors)"| Open
        Open -->|"Cooldown timer expires (30s)"| HalfOpen
        HalfOpen -->|"Canary probe succeeds"| Closed
        HalfOpen -->|"Canary probe fails"| Open
    end

    classDef closedState fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px,color:#14532d;
    classDef openState fill:#ffe4e6,stroke:#e11d48,stroke-width:2px,color:#881337;
    classDef halfOpenState fill:#fef3c7,stroke:#d97706,stroke-width:2px,color:#78350f;

    class Closed closedState;
    class Open openState;
    class HalfOpen halfOpenState;
```

</DiagramExpand>

---

## Standard Configuration Parameters

Enterprise mobile applications must initialize circuit breakers using standardized parameters:

| Configuration Parameter | Recommended Baseline | Operational Behavior |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `failureThreshold` | `3 consecutive failures` | Number of 5xx errors or socket timeouts before transitioning from **Closed** to **Open**. |
| `requestTimeoutMs` | `5,000 ms (5 seconds)` | Maximum duration for an individual network request before triggering a timeout error. |
| `resetTimeoutMs` | `30,000 ms (30 seconds)` | Duration the breaker remains in **Open** before allowing a canary probe in **Half-Open**. |
| `maxRetriesWithBackoff` | `2 retries` | Exponential backoff with jitter applied only while in **Closed** state before incrementing failures. |

---

## Implementation Across Platforms

The Circuit Breaker pattern is implemented below using standard platform concurrency primitives:

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="React Native (TypeScript)">

### Self-Contained TypeScript Circuit Breaker & Repository

```typescript
// src/core/resilience/CircuitBreaker.ts
export type BreakerState = 'CLOSED' | 'OPEN' | 'HALF_OPEN';

export interface CircuitBreakerOptions {
  failureThreshold?: number;
  resetTimeoutMs?: number;
}

export class CircuitBreaker {
  private state: BreakerState = 'CLOSED';
  private failureCount = 0;
  private lastFailureTime = 0;
  private readonly failureThreshold: number;
  private readonly resetTimeoutMs: number;

  constructor(options: CircuitBreakerOptions = {}) {
    this.failureThreshold = options.failureThreshold ?? 3;
    this.resetTimeoutMs = options.resetTimeoutMs ?? 30000;
  }

  getState(): BreakerState {
    if (this.state === 'OPEN' && Date.now() - this.lastFailureTime > this.resetTimeoutMs) {
      this.state = 'HALF_OPEN';
    }
    return this.state;
  }

  async execute<T>(
    primaryAction: () => Promise<T>,
    fallbackAction: (error: Error) => Promise<T>
  ): Promise<T> {
    const currentState = this.getState();

    if (currentState === 'OPEN') {
      return fallbackAction(new Error('Circuit breaker is OPEN. Fast-failing network call.'));
    }

    try {
      const result = await primaryAction();
      if (currentState === 'HALF_OPEN') {
        this.state = 'CLOSED';
        this.failureCount = 0;
      }
      return result;
    } catch (err: unknown) {
      this.failureCount++;
      this.lastFailureTime = Date.now();

      if (this.failureCount >= this.failureThreshold) {
        this.state = 'OPEN';
      }

      const error = err instanceof Error ? err : new Error(String(err));
      return fallbackAction(error);
    }
