## Flag Hygiene & Retirement Policy

Feature flags must not remain in the codebase indefinitely. Stale flags increase code complexity and inflate test matrices:

| Flag Category | Recommended Lifecycle | Engineering Action |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Release Flags** | Deprecate post-100% stable rollout | Remove flag checks, purge legacy fallback code, archive flag. |
| **Kill Switches** | Permanent (Audited periodically) | Retain for high-risk integrations; verify fallback path regularly. |
| **Experiment Flags** | Remove upon experiment completion | Merge winning variant; delete alternative paths. |

---

## Architectural Non-Negotiables

<Aside type="danger" title="FEATURE FLAG ANTI-PATTERNS">
The following practices are strictly prohibited:

1. **Direct Core Unleash Connections:** Mobile applications must never connect directly to the core Unleash database or management server. All connections must route through the Unleash Proxy Edge.
2. **Blocking App Launch:** Never block app cold start waiting for a network flag response. Always initialize synchronously from local storage cache.
3. **Omitting Fallback Defaults:** Every `isEnabled()` evaluation must specify an explicit, safe fallback boolean to guarantee proper offline behavior.
4. **Random Stickiness:** Never configure `random` stickiness in mobile rollouts, as user experience would fluctuate between app launches.
5. **Non-Standard Flag Naming:** Flags must adhere to the naming schema: `mobile.[platform].[domain].[feature_name]` (e.g. `mobile.all.checkout.one_click_pay`).
</Aside>

---

