import { Tabs, TabItem, Card, CardGrid, Aside } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import DiagramExpand from './DiagramExpand.astro';

<Aside type="tip" title="OFFLINE-FIRST MANDATE">
Mobile applications operate in an offline-first data lifecycle where network disconnections are treated as standard operational states. UI views render immediately from local storage, and mutations are guaranteed through an idempotent Outbox synchronization queue.
</Aside>

## Zero-Spinner Mobile Philosophy

Traditional mobile architectures follow a network-first approach where opening a screen displays a loading spinner waiting on an HTTP response. In low-connectivity environments, users experience high latency and UI freezes.

Enterprise applications enforce a **Zero-Spinner** architecture:
1. **Instant Render from Local Storage:** Screens render immediately (&lt;16ms) using cached data from the local database upon cold start.
2. **Background Network Reconciliation:** Remote queries execute asynchronously in the background. Reactive data streams (`StateFlow`, `@Published`, React hooks) update the UI seamlessly when new records arrive.
3. **Optimistic Local Mutations:** User actions (such as order submissions or status updates) apply to local stores immediately and enqueue to a durable **Outbox Queue**.

---

## Tiered Local Storage Architecture

Enterprise applications must select local storage engines based on data structure and query patterns:

| Storage Tier | Recommended Technology | Ideal Use Cases | Security Requirement |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Secure Key-Value** | MMKV / SecureStore | User preferences, session flags, feature toggle cache | AES-128 encryption with hardware-backed keys |
| **Relational Database** | Room (Android) / CoreData or SwiftData (iOS) | Complex entity graphs, offline sync outbox, indexed search | File-level hardware encryption (SQLCipher where required) |
| **Hardware Enclave** | KeyStore (Android) / Keychain (iOS) | OAuth tokens, refresh tokens, cryptographic signing keys | Hardware enclave backing with biometric gating |

---

## Outbox Synchronization Pattern

To guarantee that user mutations are never lost during cellular dead zones, applications decouple user interactions from immediate network calls using the **Outbox Pattern**:

<DiagramExpand title="OUTBOX SYNCHRONIZATION PATTERN">

```mermaid
