import { Aside } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import DiagramExpand from './DiagramExpand.astro';

<Aside type="tip" title="SOURCE CONTROL MANDATE">
All mobile repositories enforce strict GitFlow branch isolation, peer reviews, automated CI quality gates, and Conventional Commits. Direct pushes to integration and production branches are blocked by branch protection policies.
</Aside>

## Core Engineering Principles

Every engineer contributing to enterprise mobile repositories adheres to these foundational principles:
* **Maintain Branch Stability:** Production code in `master` must always represent deployable, audited software.
* **Isolate Feature Development:** All work occurs in dedicated, short-lived feature or bugfix branches.
* **Pull Request Enforcement:** Code enters shared branches exclusively through tracked Pull Requests.
* **Peer Review Mandate:** Every PR requires approval from qualified peers before merging.
* **Automated CI Gates:** Automated test suites, lint checks, and security scans must pass completely.
* **Traceable Commits:** Commit messages and branch names reference corresponding Jira issue keys.

---

## Standard Repository Branches

Enterprise mobile repositories utilize a standardized branch hierarchy:

| Branch Name | Operational Role | Protection Policy | Target Merge Policy |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `master` / `prod` | Authoritative production release code. Every commit is tagged (`vYYYY.MM.build`). | Direct push blocked. Strict branch protection. | Squash and merge from release or hotfix branches. |
| `develop` | Primary integration branch for active sprint development. | Direct push blocked. Requires CI pass + review approval. | Squash and merge from feature and bugfix branches. |
| `release-*` | Release candidate staging branch (`release-2026.10`). Only bug fixes and version bumps. | Push restricted to release engineers. | Merges into `master` and back-merges into `develop`. |
| `feature-*` | Temporary branch for new capabilities (`feature-checkout-flow`). | Open to assigned engineers. | Deleted upon merge into `develop`. |
| `bugfix-*` | Temporary branch for non-urgent defect resolution (`bugfix-cart-recalculation`). | Open to assigned engineers. | Deleted upon merge into `develop`. |
| `hotfix-*` | Emergency patch branched directly from `master` (`hotfix-session-crash`). | Restricted to hotfix squads. | Mandatory dual-merge into `master` and `develop`. |

---

## Feature Branch Workflow

### Creating a Feature Branch
Always branch from the latest state of the `develop` branch:

```bash
# 1. Ensure develop is up to date
git checkout develop
git pull origin develop

# 2. Cut a new feature branch
git checkout -b feature-PROJ-1234-order-tracking
```

### Branch Naming Conventions
Branch names must follow structured prefixes incorporating the issue tracking key:

| Branch Type | Syntax Format | Example |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **New Feature** | `feature-[JIRA_KEY]-[short-description]` | `feature-MOB-4120-biometric-login` |
| **Bug Fix** | `bugfix-[JIRA_KEY]-[short-description]` | `bugfix-MOB-4155-cart-total-precision` |
| **Emergency Hotfix** | `hotfix-[JIRA_KEY]-[short-description]` | `hotfix-MOB-4201-auth-token-refresh` |
| **Technical Debt** | `chore-[JIRA_KEY]-[short-description]` | `chore-MOB-4089-upgrade-gradle-wrapper` |

---

## Development Flows

### Feature Development Flow
Standard sprint tasks follow a sequential path from local development to `develop` integration:

<DiagramExpand title="FEATURE DEVELOPMENT WORKFLOW">

